        <P322>BRIDG</P322>
        <P325>A series of actions among molecules in a cell that leads to a certain end point or cell function.</P325>
        <P325>A series of actions among molecules in or associated with a cell that leads to a certain product or a change in a cell. Such a pathway can trigger the assembly of new molecules, such as a fat or protein. Pathways can also turn genes on and off, or spur a cell to move. [Adapted from: http://www.genome.gov/27530687]  EXAMPLE(S):  Notch signaling pathway  OTHER NAME(S):  NOTE(S):</P325>
        <P366>Biochemical_Pathway</P366>
        <P90>Biochemical Pathway</P90>
        <P90>Metabolic Network</P90>
        <P90>Pathway</P90>
        <P90>Pathways</P90>
        <P90>molecular pathway</P90>
        <P95>2010 Biochemical Pathway branch update.</P95>
        <P97>A series of biochemical reactions which occurs in a known sequence and can result in all or part of the initiation, progression or completion of a biological process or function.</P97>
        <rdfs:label>Biochemical Pathway</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
